1
00:00:01,760 --> 00:00:05,760
happened to be with him growled so threateningly, that they accepted his

2
00:00:05,760 --> 00:00:10,199
explanation without further questions.

3
00:00:10,199 --> 00:00:13,159
Chapter 6

4
00:00:14,239 --> 00:00:20,319
All that year the animals worked like slaves, but they were happy in their work. They

5
00:00:20,319 --> 00:00:25,600
grudged no effort or sacrifice, well aware that everything that they did was

6
00:00:25,600 --> 00:00:30,359
for the benefit of themselves and those of their kind who would come after them,

7
00:00:30,359 --> 00:00:36,760
and not for a pack of idle, thieving human beings.

8
00:00:36,760 --> 00:00:42,079
Throughout the spring and summer, they worked a 60-hour week, and in August

9
00:00:42,079 --> 00:00:47,720
Napoleon announced that there would be work on Sunday afternoons as well.

10
00:00:47,720 --> 00:00:53,279
This work was strictly voluntary, but any animal who absented himself from

11
00:00:53,279 --> 00:00:57,680
it would have his rations reduced by half.

12
00:00:57,680 --> 00:01:03,399
Even so, it was found necessary to leave certain tasks undone.

13
00:01:03,399 --> 00:01:08,159
The harvest was a little less successful than in the previous year, and two

14
00:01:08,159 --> 00:01:13,439
fields, which should have been sown with roots in the early summer, were not sown

15
00:01:13,439 --> 00:01:17,319
because the plowing had not been completed early enough.

16
00:01:17,319 --> 00:01:22,600
It was possible to foresee that the coming winter would be a hard one.

17
00:01:22,600 --> 00:01:26,880
The windmill presented unexpected difficulties.

18
00:01:26,880 --> 00:01:31,000
There was a good quarry of limestone on the farm, and plenty of sand and cement

19
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:36,439
had been found in one of the outhouses, so that all the materials for building were at hand.

20
00:01:36,439 --> 00:01:40,920
But the problem the animals could not at first solve was how to break up the

21
00:01:40,920 --> 00:01:44,640
stone into pieces of suitable size.

22
00:01:44,640 --> 00:01:50,560
There seemed no way of doing this except with picks and crowbars, which no animal

23
00:01:50,560 --> 00:01:55,319
could use because no animal could stand on his hind legs.

24
00:01:55,319 --> 00:02:01,720
Only after weeks of vain effort did the right idea occur to somebody.

25
00:02:01,720 --> 00:02:06,079
Namely, to utilize the force of gravity.

26
00:02:06,079 --> 00:02:11,520
Huge boulders, far too big to be used as they were, were lying all over the bed

27
00:02:11,520 --> 00:02:13,159
of the quarry.

28
00:02:13,159 --> 00:02:19,640
The animals lashed ropes round these, and then, all together, cows, horses,

29
00:02:19,640 --> 00:02:25,000
sheep, any animal that could lay hold of the rope, even the pigs sometimes joined

30
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:26,880
in at critical moments.

31
00:02:26,880 --> 00:02:31,520
They dragged them with desperate slowness up the slope to the top of the

32
00:02:31,520 --> 00:02:37,399
quarry, where they were toppled over the edge to shatter to pieces below.

33
00:02:37,399 --> 00:02:42,680
Transporting the stone, when it was once broken, was comparatively simple.

34
00:02:42,680 --> 00:02:48,000
The horses carried it off in cartloads, the sheep dragged single blocks,

35
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:55,079
even Muriel and Benjamin yoked themselves into an old governess cart and did their share.

36
00:02:55,079 --> 00:03:02,319
By late summer, a sufficient store of stone had accumulated, and then the building began

37
00:03:02,319 --> 00:03:05,720
under the superintendence of the pigs.

38
00:03:05,720 --> 00:03:09,239
But it was a slow, laborious process.

39
00:03:09,239 --> 00:03:13,800
Frequently, it took a whole day of exhausting effort to drag a single

40
00:03:13,800 --> 00:03:18,079
boulder to the top of the quarry, and sometimes, when it was pushed over

41
00:03:18,079 --> 00:03:21,640
the edge, it failed to break.

42
00:03:21,640 --> 00:03:26,159
Nothing could have been achieved without Boxer, whose strength seemed equal to

43
00:03:26,159 --> 00:03:30,119
that of all the rest of the animals put together.

44
00:03:30,119 --> 00:03:34,439
When the boulder began to slip and the animals cried out in despair at finding

45
00:03:34,439 --> 00:03:40,079
themselves dragged down the hill, it was always Boxer who strained himself

46
00:03:40,079 --> 00:03:44,159
against the rope and brought the boulder to a stop.

47
00:03:44,159 --> 00:03:50,439
To see him toiling up the slope inch by inch, his breath coming fast, the tips

48
00:03:50,439 --> 00:03:56,520
of his hooves clawing at the ground, and his great sides matted with sweat,

49
00:03:56,520 --> 00:04:00,359
filled everyone with admiration.

50
00:04:00,359 --> 00:04:05,000
Clover warned him sometimes to be careful not to overstrain himself,

51
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:08,600
but Boxer would never listen to her.

52
00:04:08,600 --> 00:04:17,520
His two slogans, >> I will work harder and Napoleon is always right,

53
00:04:17,520 --> 00:04:21,399
>> seemed to him a sufficient answer to all problems.

54
00:04:21,399 --> 00:04:24,520
He had made arrangements with the cockerel to call him three-quarters of

55
00:04:24,520 --> 00:04:28,479
an hour earlier in the mornings instead of half an hour,

56
00:04:28,479 --> 00:04:32,640
and in his spare moments, of which there were not many nowadays,

57
00:04:32,640 --> 00:04:37,680
he would go alone to the quarry, collect a load of broken stone, and drag it down

58
00:04:37,680 --> 00:04:40,760
to the site of the windmill unassisted.

59
00:04:40,760 --> 00:04:46,279
The animals were not badly off throughout that summer in spite of the hardness of their work.

60
00:04:46,279 --> 00:04:49,720
If they had no more food than they had had in Jones's day,

61
00:04:49,720 --> 00:04:52,399
at least they did not have less.

62
00:04:52,399 --> 00:04:56,359
The advantage of only having to feed themselves and not having to support

63
00:04:56,359 --> 00:05:01,840
five extravagant human beings as well was so great that it would have taken a

64
00:05:01,840 --> 00:05:05,159
lot of failures to outweigh it.

65
00:05:05,159 --> 00:05:11,840
And in many ways, the animal method of doing things was more efficient and saved labor.

66
00:05:11,840 --> 00:05:16,279
Such jobs as weeding, for instance, could be done with a thoroughness

67
00:05:16,279 --> 00:05:19,239
impossible to human beings.

68
00:05:19,239 --> 00:05:24,960
And again, since no animal now stole, it was unnecessary to fence off pasture

69
00:05:24,960 --> 00:05:31,199
from arable land, which saved a lot of labor on the upkeep of hedges and gates.

70
00:05:31,199 --> 00:05:39,720
Nevertheless, as the summer wore on, various unforeseen shortages began to make themselves felt.

71
00:05:39,720 --> 00:05:46,560
There was need of paraffin oil, nails, string, dog biscuits, and iron for the

72
00:05:46,560 --> 00:05:51,159
horses' shoes, none of which could be produced on the farm.

73
00:05:51,159 --> 00:05:56,279
Later, there would also be need for seeds and artificial manures, besides

74
00:05:56,279 --> 00:06:01,399
various tools, and finally, the machinery for the windmill.

75
00:06:01,399 --> 00:06:06,079
How these were to be procured, no one was able to imagine.

76
00:06:06,079 --> 00:06:10,000
One Sunday morning, when the animals assembled to receive their orders,

77
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:14,680
Napoleon announced that he had decided upon a new policy.

78
00:06:14,680 --> 00:06:20,319
From now onwards, Animal Farm would engage in trade with the neighboring farms.

79
00:06:20,319 --> 00:06:25,239
Not, of course, for any commercial purpose, but simply in order to obtain

80
00:06:25,239 --> 00:06:29,039
certain materials which were urgently necessary.

81
00:06:29,039 --> 00:06:34,119
"The needs of the windmill must override everything else," he said.

82
00:06:34,119 --> 00:06:38,399
He was therefore making arrangements to sell a stack of hay and part of the

83
00:06:38,399 --> 00:06:43,520
current year's wheat crop, and later on, if more money were needed, it would have

84
00:06:43,520 --> 00:06:50,039
to be made up by the sale of eggs, for which there was always a market in Willingdon.

85
00:06:50,039 --> 00:06:54,680
"The hens," said Napoleon, "should welcome this sacrifice as their own

86
00:06:54,680 --> 00:06:58,840
special contribution towards the building of the windmill."

87
00:06:58,840 --> 00:07:03,479
Once again, the animals were conscious of a vague uneasiness.

88
00:07:03,479 --> 00:07:09,319
Never to have any dealings with human beings, never to engage in trade, never

89
00:07:09,319 --> 00:07:11,520
to make use of money.

90
00:07:11,520 --> 00:07:16,840
Had not these been among the earliest resolutions passed at that first

91
00:07:16,840 --> 00:07:21,319
triumphant meeting after Jones was expelled.

92
00:07:21,319 --> 00:07:26,199
All the animals remembered passing such resolutions, or at least they thought

93
00:07:26,199 --> 00:07:28,560
that they remembered it.

94
00:07:28,560 --> 00:07:33,399
The four young pigs who had protested when Napoleon abolished the meetings

95
00:07:33,399 --> 00:07:38,359
raised their voices timidly, but they were promptly silenced by a

96
00:07:38,359 --> 00:07:41,800
tremendous growling from the dogs.

97
00:07:41,800 --> 00:07:48,600
Then, as usual, the sheep broke into >> Four legs good, two legs bad.

98
00:07:48,600 --> 00:07:52,159
>> And the momentary awkwardness was smoothed over.

99
00:07:52,159 --> 00:07:56,399
Finally, Napoleon raised his trotter for silence and announced that he had

100
00:07:56,399 --> 00:07:59,239
already made all the arrangements.

101
00:07:59,239 --> 00:08:02,720
There would be no need for any of the animals to come in contact with human

102
00:08:02,720 --> 00:08:07,079
beings, which would clearly be most undesirable.

103
00:08:07,079 --> 00:08:11,560
He intended to take the whole burden upon his own shoulders.

104
00:08:11,560 --> 00:08:16,039
A Mr. Whymper, a solicitor living in Willingdon, had agreed to act as

105
00:08:16,039 --> 00:08:21,119
intermediary between Animal Farm and the outside world, and would visit the farm

106
00:08:21,119 --> 00:08:24,920
every Monday morning to receive his instructions.

107
00:08:24,920 --> 00:08:28,000
Napoleon ended his speech with his usual cry of
